   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="FR">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="FR">The Association des Employeurs du Burundi (AEB) adapted its strategy and internal processes in response to a changing business environment and the training needs of members mainly in OSH. OSH is a major issue for AEB members and OSH awareness in companies is basic and limited. AEB responded through an adapted OSH training for companies and used the knowledge (and company inputs) gained through the training to respond to the emerging OSH policy landscape. AEB implemented the translated and customized version of the ILO&#x2019;s Essentials in Occupational Safety and Health (EOSH) training for its members. A Training of Trainers (ToT) took place (March/May 2022) and was attended by 14 trainers from AEB. AEB held two subsequent trainings took place in Nov/Dec 2022 (Kayanza, Makambo provinces). A technical training was provided on OSH legislation for 90 human resources managers from AEB members (June 2022).  AEB also provided business resilience training entitled Sustainable and Resilient Enterprises (SURE) for SMEs. These efforts contributed greatly to the development of a national policy on OSH. A tripartite workshop held in April 2022 resulted in the formulation of a roadmap for a national OSH policy. AEB&#x2019;s involvement in policy work showcased the value of its training initiatives, emphasizing its role as a policy influencer with insights into shaping of OSH legislation. Throughout March 2022-Feb 2023, AEB held seven follow-up meetings with the Ministry in advance of the formal agreement and launch of the national policy. In collaboration with Burundi&apos;s apex trade union organization, COSYBU, AEB developed a guide on managing social dialogue at company levels, incorporating OSH protocols. They also conducted a series of sensitization seminars (Q2-3 2022) at company levels across the country (using the Guide), covering about 160 companies. AEB used the materials effectively in media briefings and in dialogue with the government and unions throughout the biennium. With RBSA funding, the ILO provided support to AEB in developing OSH and resilience training. This assistance encompassed the translation and adaption of the ILO&#x2019;s EOSH training to the Burundi context, followed by the support for the EOSH Training of Trainers (ToT) (28th March to May 27th 2022) to support the training&#x2019;s sustainability. Additionally, the ILO supported a technical training on OSH legislation for AEB members. Furthermore, the ILO, in partnership with ITC-ILO, facilitated a Training of Trainers (ToT) of the business resilience training SURE for SMEs. The SURE training took place from April/May 2022 and covered 12 trainers. The ILO supported the tripartite partners in the development of a national policy on OSH. This assistance included organizing a tripartite workshop (29th of April 2022) to develop a roadmap for the national OSH policy. Additionally, the ILO supported the formal agreement and launch of the national policy. The ILO provided support to AEB in collaboration with Burundi&apos;s apex trade union organization, COSYBU, to develop a guide on the management of social dialogue at company levels which included OSH protocols. The ILO provided (throughout 2022/23) institutional support comprising facilities for its in-house conference room including IT equipment to be able to organize and participate in virtual sessions. ITC-ILO also provided (physical) training support (the flagship EBMO Master course) to one senior AEB staff to support follow through for the training offer (November 2023).</narrative>
   </description>
